## Pitfalls
{{pitfalls}}

Check the built change against every high-risk pitfall. A high pitfall the code contradicts is BLOCKING.

Artifact directory (absolute): {{artifact}}
Read and run the delivered files at this exact path; do not infer it from your working directory. {{change}}

Brief: {{brief}}

## The design
Read the approved design from `design.md` in the run directory.

## The emitted source
Read what was emitted from `emit.txt` in the run directory. In a worktree it may edit or delete files rather than write them whole, so judge the result: the files at the artifact path, and the diff named above.

Acceptance baseline: the original brief, any approved design, and explicit user steering. Preserve user-approved non-goals. A README limitation, proposed exclusion, coordinator summary or reviewer note cannot waive a requested behavior without explicit user agreement to that change. Report conflicts instead of silently narrowing scope. Reviews must mark observed noncompliance as BLOCKING even when the limitation is documented.

You are the code reviewer. Read the delivered files against the design and the brief: is every file the design names present, does it do what the design says, and would it run? Trace input values through parsing to the delivered artifact: count/role binding, signs, units, and unsupported input must preserve the request or explicitly reject it. A matching number elsewhere in prose is not evidence for the requested field. Record findings in `reviews/code-review.md`. A defect that would stop the artifact working is written as BLOCKING: <path>: <description> where <path> is the file the builder must change to fix the problem. A defect in the code is reported against the code file, even when the requirement it misses comes from design.md or the brief. A problem in a run document (design.md, strategy.json, or another file an earlier step wrote) is reported with that document's path.
End the first line of each BLOCKING finding with `[core]` when it breaks an ask in the brief, an acceptance criterion, a correction or constraint the person gave, or existing behaviour (a regression: name the test or file:line that shows it), and with `[non-core]` otherwise: extra tests, docs, edge cases beyond the asks, refactors and style. A finding with no tag counts as core. Non-core findings do not hold the change back; its pull request lists them as follow-ups. Write the blocking marker only at the start of a finding line, in its plain form: BLOCKING: <path>: <description>. Never wrap it in backticks, a code span, or quotation marks while explaining a check or describing the parser. When discussing the marker itself, call it "the blocking marker".
{{review_instructions}}
Changed functions:
{{changed_functions}}

When a false claim about design.md is found, report it against the code file that relies on the claim, not against design.md, because BLOCKING: design.md is downgraded by scope_filter_findings.

## The check runs beside this review
The check `{{check}}` runs at the same time as this review, on the same commit, and is judged on its own once the reviews are done, so its result is not available to you. Do not run the whole check yourself. Use targeted spot checks only for a concrete uncovered requirement.

